/-!
# The δ-dimension drop at the zero scheme of a regular section, read on a chart

In Situation 0EDT let `X/B` be a good algebraic space with `dim_δ(X) ≤ k + 1`, let `ℒ` be an
invertible `𝒪_X`-module and let `s` be a **regular** global section of `ℒ`.  This file proves
that the chart of the zero scheme `Z(s)` of Stacks 0845 inside an affine étale chart `V` of `X`
has δ-dimension at most `k`: the closed subscheme of `V` cut out by the evaluation ideal of `s`
drops the δ-dimension of `V` by at least one.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.exists_specializes_ne_of_mem_nonZeroDivisors_maximalIdeal`: a point of
  a scheme whose local ring has a nonzerodivisor in its maximal ideal has a proper
  generalization.
* `Stacks.Chow.schemeDeltaDimension_comp_le_of_forall_exists_nonZeroDivisors_stalk`: a closed
  subscheme at each of whose points the ambient local ring carries a nonzerodivisor in its
  maximal ideal has δ-dimension one less than a bound for the ambient scheme.
* `Stacks.Chow.schemeDeltaDimension_subschemeι_ofIdealTop_le_of_mem_nonZeroDivisors`: the same for
  the closed subscheme of an affine scheme cut out by an ideal containing a nonzerodivisor.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_isRegular_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_eq_span_of_free_generator`:
  on a chart carrying a free generator of `ℒ` the evaluation ideal of a regular section is
  generated by a single nonzerodivisor.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.schemeDeltaDimension_subschemeι_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_le`: **the
  δ-dimension bound `dim_δ(Z(s)) ≤ k` of Stacks 0EQL, read on the chart**, from `dim_δ(X) ≤ k+1`
  and regularity of `s`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_germ_generates_stalk_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_of_free_generator`:
  the local equation of `Z(s)` at a chart point on it — a global regular function on the chart
  whose germ generates the stalk of the chart ideal sheaf, is a nonzerodivisor, and is not a
  unit.

## Implementation notes

### The drop itself is a statement about generalizations

A dimension function in the sense of Stacks 02I8 (`TopologicalSpace.DimensionFunction`) is
strictly decreasing along nontrivial specializations.  So to push a pointwise bound `δ ≤ k + 1`
on `V` down to `δ ≤ k` at a point `v` of the closed subscheme it is enough to exhibit *one* point
of `V` that properly generalizes `v`; this is
`Stacks.Chow.schemeDeltaDimension_comp_le_of_forall_exists_nonZeroDivisors_stalk`, which then
feeds `Stacks.Chow.schemeDeltaDimension_le_of_forall_relativeDimension_le`.

A proper generalization exists exactly because the local equation is a nonzerodivisor.  The germ of
`l₀(s)` at `v` lies in the maximal ideal of `𝒪_{V,v}` (that is what it means for `v` to lie on
`Z(s)`) and is a nonzerodivisor there, localization carrying nonzerodivisors to nonzerodivisors
through flatness (`Module.Flat.isSMulRegular_of_nonZeroDivisors` over
`IsAffineOpen.isLocalization_stalk`).  An element of a minimal prime is a zerodivisor
(`notMem_nonZeroDivisors_of_mem_mem_minimalPrimes`), so a minimal prime of `𝒪_{V,v}` — one exists
below the maximal ideal by `Ideal.exists_minimalPrimes_le` — is *strictly* below the maximal ideal,
and `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.range_fromSpecStalk` turns it into a point of `V` generalizing `v`.
That point differs from `v` because `Spec 𝒪_{V,v} ⟶ V` is a preimmersion, hence injective on
points, and `v` is the image of the closed point.

No Noetherian hypothesis is used for this: `Ideal.exists_minimalPrimes_le` and
`notMem_nonZeroDivisors_of_mem_mem_minimalPrimes` hold over any commutative ring.

